#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 463 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 A sample-accurate counter that counts audio samples
while running. It can be started and stopped by a control signal (on
a 0→1 transition the counter starts \, on a 1→0 transition it stops)
\, and its current value can be reset or set explicitly. The output
is the current sample number since the last reset.;
#X text 240 184 Counter overflow and integer precision:;
#X text 250 209 • 32-bit float (single precision): exact integer counting
is guaranteed only up to 2^24 = 16 \, 777 \, 216 samples. Above this
value \, consecutive integers can no longer be represented — the spacing
between representable values grows larger than 1 \, so the counter
may appear to "stick" or skip samples. At a sample rate of 44100 Hz
this limit is reached after approximately 6 minutes and 20 seconds
of continuous counting.;
#X text 250 340 • 64-bit float (double precision): exact integer counting
is guaranteed up to 2^53 = 9 \, 007 \, 199 \, 254 \, 740 \, 992 samples.
This is many orders of magnitude beyond any practical audio session
\, so with double-precision Pd the counter is effectively exact for
any realistic use.;
#X text 240 425 Which precision is used depends on how Pd itself is
compiled: the vanilla Pd distribution typically uses single-precision
floats \, while CEAMMC distributions are available in both single and
double precision. If you plan to run a very long counter \, prefer
a double-precision build.;
